01:02:27,692 --> 01:02:33,692
Crimson Tide is a 1995 American submarine action thriller film directed by
Tony Scott and produced by Don Simpson and Jerry Bruckheimer.

15
01:06:55,384 --> 01:07:01,384
It takes place during a period of political turmoil in Russia, in which
ultranationalists threaten to launch nuclear missiles at the United States and Japan.

16
01:11:23,076 --> 01:11:29,076
The film focuses on a clash of wills between the
seasoned commanding officer of a U.S.

17
01:15:50,769 --> 01:15:56,769
nuclear missile submarine (Gene Hackman) and his new executive officer (or XO, played by Denzel
Washington), arising from conflicting interpretations of an order to launch their missiles.

18
01:20:18,461 --> 01:20:24,461
The story inadvertently parallels a real incident
during the Cuban Missile Crisis.

19
01:24:46,153 --> 01:24:52,153
Crimson Tide was released by Buena Vista Pictures Distribution on May 12, 1995.

20
01:29:13,846 --> 01:29:19,846
The film received mostly positive reviews from critics and
grossed $157.4 million against a $53 million budget.

21
01:33:41,538 --> 01:33:47,538
Hans Zimmer, who scored the film, won a Grammy Award for the main theme, which
heavily uses synthesizers instead of traditional orchestral instruments.

22
01:38:09,230 --> 01:38:15,230
An extended cut, which incorporates seven minutes of deleted scenes, was released on DVD in
2006, while the 2008 Blu-ray release only includes the theatrical version.

23
01:42:36,923 --> 01:42:42,923
In a 2025 interview, Bruckheimer mentioned that he was developing a sequel
and was hopeful that Washington would reprise his role.

24
01:47:04,615 --> 01:47:10,615
The film explores themes of mutiny, submarine,
missile, embassy, nuclear missile.

25
01:51:32,307 --> 01:51:38,307
The tone is emotional and intense.
